hdMoonray: USD 26 IsSupported override + pxr_python link for cmd binaries

USD 26 added a new pure-virtual `IsSupported` overload on
`HdRendererPlugin`:

  virtual bool IsSupported(
      HdRendererCreateArgs const &rendererCreateArgs,
      std::string *reasonWhyNot = nullptr) const = 0;

The pre-26 `IsSupported(bool gpuEnabled = true)` overload became
deprecated and non-virtual. hdMoonray's `HdMoonrayRendererPlugin`
and `HdMoonrayRendererDebugPlugin` only overrode the legacy form
→ the class stays abstract under USD 26 → `new T` fails inside
`HfPluginRegistry::_CreatePlugin()`.

Fix: add the new override gated on `PXR_VERSION >= 2603` so older
USD builds still see the legacy form:

  #if PXR_VERSION >= 2603
      bool IsSupported(pxr::HdRendererCreateArgs const&,
                       std::string* = nullptr) const override { return true; }
  #elif PXR_VERSION >= 2302
      bool IsSupported(bool gpuEnabled = true) const override { return true; }
  #else
      bool IsSupported() const override { return true; }
  #endif

Also: the `hd_render` and `hd_usd2rdl` standalone command binaries
need to link `libusd_python.so` directly (USD 26 moved its bundled
boost::python implementation into a separate library; previously it
was statically pulled in by other USD libs). Adds a lowercase
`python` entry to their `target_link_libraries`.

DEPENDENCY on the cmake-shim: the lowercase `python` target name
matches an `IMPORTED` target the pxr cmake shim creates for
`libusd_python.so` (see `building/gaffer/setup-gaffer-deps.sh`,
which generates `pxrTargets.cmake` with one IMPORTED target per
`libusd_*.so` discovered in the dep tarball, naming each by its
`libusd_<name>` suffix). Without the shim, `target_link_libraries(...
python)` fails to resolve. The shim is set up unconditionally by
the gaffer-release preset, so this dependency is always satisfied
in our build path; flagging it here for upstream-submission
preparedness (an upstream variant should use a CMake-version-gated
fully-qualified target like `pxr::python` and document its
availability across USD versions).

Apply path: hdMoonray submodule (scope: hdMoonray).

